 Since the octagon is equiangular, we know that each internal angle is $135^{\circ}$. Split the octagon into six triangles with the diagonals as shown, as shown in the figure. Let the area of $\triangle O A_{1} B_{1}, \triangle B_{1} A_{2} C_{2}, \triangle C_{2} A_{3} D_{3}, \triangle D_{3} A_{4} E_{4}, \triangle E_{4} F_{4} G_{4}, \triangle G_{4} O A_{1}$ be $S_{1}, S_{2}, S_{3}, S_{4}, S_{5}, S_{6}$ respectively. By calculating the area at each vertex, we can find the following equalities: 
$$
\begin{array}{l}
\ S_{1}+\frac{1}{2}=S_{6}, \quad S_{2}+\frac{1}{2}=S_{1}, \\
\ S_{3}+2=S_{2}, \quad S_{4}+2=S_{3}, \\
\ S_{5}+4=S_{4}, \quad S_{6}+4=S_{5}
\end{array}
$$
Sum the six equalities, we get $S_{1}+S_{2}+S_{3}+S_{4}+S_{5}+S_{6}+18= S_{1}+S_{2}+S_{3}+S_{4}+S_{5}+S_{6}+4$.
Therefore, the area of the equiangular octagon is $14$.</think> <answer> $14$</answer>

